User Mode vs Kernel Mode

This is one of the most important OS concepts because it explains:

Why applications can't directly access hardware
How system security works
What happens during a system call
Why modern operating systems don't crash easily when an application crashes
First Understand the Problem

Suppose there is no restriction on applications.

Then Chrome could:

Delete SSD
Format Disk
Modify RAM
Shutdown System
Access Other Processes

Or a virus could directly control hardware.

This would be disastrous.

Solution: CPU Modes

Modern CPUs have different execution modes.

The two most important are:

User Mode
Kernel Mode

Think of them as different permission levels.

Real-Life Example: Airport

Imagine an airport.

Normal Passenger

Can:

✔ Sit in waiting area

✔ Buy food

✔ Board flight

Cannot:

❌ Enter control tower

❌ Control airplanes

❌ Access security systems

Air Traffic Controller

Can:

✔ Control aircraft

✔ Access critical systems

✔ Issue commands

Similarly:

Passenger = User Mode
Controller = Kernel Mode
What is User Mode?

User Mode is the restricted mode where normal applications execute.

Examples:

Chrome
Spotify
VS Code
Notepad
WhatsApp

run in User Mode.

Characteristics of User Mode

Applications can:

✔ Perform calculations

✔ Display windows

✔ Process user input

✔ Request OS services

Applications cannot:

❌ Access hardware directly

❌ Modify kernel memory

❌ Execute privileged instructions

❌ Access other processes freely

Example

Chrome wants to save a PDF.

Can Chrome directly write to SSD?

No.

Chrome must request the OS.

Flow:

Chrome
   ↓
System Call
   ↓
Kernel
   ↓
SSD
Why User Mode Exists?

Main reason:

Protection

If Chrome crashes:

Chrome Crash

OS continues running.

Without User Mode:

Chrome Crash
      ↓
Entire System Crash

Possible.

What is Kernel Mode?

Kernel Mode is the privileged mode where the Operating System kernel executes.

Examples of kernel components:

Scheduler
Memory Manager
File System
Device Drivers
Process Manager

Kernel Mode has full access.

Can:

✔ Access hardware

✔ Access RAM

✔ Execute privileged instructions

✔ Manage processes

✔ Control devices

Think:

Kernel Mode = Administrator
User Mode = Normal User
